From d5dc05ec1a93d827b8249a0d7f80b62ab0c50058 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 08:56:27 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 150/174] feat: two 1D curves on the CineCam Grade - a master
 tone curve and Sat vs Sat (S10 item 3b)

The shape a CDL cannot make, and the last thing the live desk was missing: one master
(luma) curve applied identically to all three channels, and a Sat vs Sat curve that
scales saturation as a function of how saturated a pixel already was.

CHAIN POSITION - after the ASC CDL, before the LMT. Not invented: that is the fixed
processing order every non-Freestyle grading mode in Pomfort Livegrade enforces (input
transform -> primary adjustments = CDL and saturation -> secondary adjustments = curves
-> output transform), and its ACES Advanced mode is literally this node set. Livegrade's
own reason for fixing the order is ours too: it is what makes a CDL + LUT export
possible. With nothing in front of the CDL, the .cdl sidecar shipped in d49e019f still
describes the FRONT of the chain and still reproduces in Resolve as node 1, with the
curves as node 2. Master curve before Sat vs Sat, because a per-channel tone curve
changes chroma as a side effect and the saturation trim should see the final tonality.

AUTHORING REPRESENTATION - five reflected floats per curve, not a curve widget. The
widget route was traced all the way and rejected on evidence: a reflected
ISplineEvaluator really does draw a curve row here (Schematyc hands any type with its
own Serialize straight to yasli; the entity inspector is the legacy yasli tree; that
tree registers PropertyRowCurve for ISplineEvaluator) - but the only CCurveEditorPanel
in the whole 5.7.1 Sandbox is built by the Particle Editor, so in the level editor the
row's edit broadcast has no receiver and the curve cannot be dragged. A spline is also
not TrackView-animatable, cannot cross the post-effect bus, and turns "neutral is an
exact identity" from a property into a measurement. Five floats are typeable, readable,
animatable, and their neutral is a literal zero.

Master curve knots sit at fixed ACEScct landmarks - the bottom of the encoding, four
stops under 18% grey, 18% grey itself, four stops over, and ACEScct 1.0 (+10.3 stops) -
and each control is an OFFSET IN STOPS, so the neutral is 0 and the read-out is the
language the desk already uses. Sat vs Sat is five multipliers at 0/25/50/75/100% of
measured saturation, neutral 1, which is Resolve's convention for that control.
Interpolation is monotone cubic Hermite (Fritsch-Carlson): it cannot overshoot, so a
steep knot does not ring around a highlight, and on collinear knots it collapses to the
straight line exactly - the identity curve is bit-exact.

SATURATION MEASURE - sat = saturate(length(c - luma709) * 2.0) in ACEScct, with the ASC
Rec.709 luma weights the CDL saturation has used since 3a, and the multiplier applied
about that same luma. It is the length of the very vector the multiplier scales, so
pushing the curve to zero produces exactly the grey the operator produces; it keeps one
meaning of "saturation" inside the component; and it is exposure-invariant, because the
space is log and the weights sum to 1, so a stop shifts c and luma alike. x = 1 is 8.8
stops of spread between the extreme channels; a strong red reads ~0.54, a near-primary
~0.96, any neutral exactly 0. The scale rides in the shader constant rather than as an
HLSL literal so the shader, the bake and the sidecar cannot disagree.

RENDERER - one 1024x1 RGBA16F texture on t5 (R = master curve, G = the multiplier),
LinearClamp on its own slot s2, uploaded by the plugin through UploadToVideoMemory with
h = 1 and released by the same name-checked, id-safe path the 3D LUTs use. Two curves in
two CHANNELS and not two rows: a two-row texture has to be sampled at the row centres
exactly or a linear sampler bleeds one curve into the other, invisibly. A whole new
float4 SceneCurveParams (grid N, master active, sat active, axis scale) because all
three SceneCDL* .w lanes are spent and SceneWBRow1/2's free lanes belong to the
white-balance step. Neither fetch is issued when its curve is neutral, so an unused
curve is free and the neutral picture is bit-identical; the stock path never reaches any
of it. The master curve is extended with slope 1 outside [0,1] rather than clamped,
because the EXR tap is downstream and a capture must keep highlight separation the
sensor recorded.

EXPORT - the ASC CDL cannot carry either curve, so they travel as their own files, once
per take and only when non-neutral (r_SceneReferredExportCurves).
<prefix>.curve_master.cube is the master curve as a Resolve 1D LUT (LUT_1D_SIZE 1024),
which applies mechanically on the node after the .cdl node. <prefix>.curves.txt carries
both curves' control points plus the exact definition of the Sat vs Sat axis, because
that curve is a multiplier indexed by a per-pixel measure and NOT a per-channel value
mapping - a .cube of it would load, apply and be silently nonsense. rec/curves joins
rec/cdl in every EXR header. Bypass Grade folds in as the neutral, exactly as it does
for the CDL.

To keep one definition of what the curve between two knots is, the knot positions, the
interpolator, the bake and the neutrality tests live in a new shared header
CryCommon/CryRenderer/SceneReferredCurves.h, included by the plugin (to bake the
texture) and by CSceneReferredExport (to write the .cube) - the same kind of shared
numeric truth RENDERER_LIGHT_UNIT_SCALE already is, rather than two evaluators to keep
in step.

All ten properties are TrackView-animatable, in a new Curves group; the Grade group's
member ids and names are untouched.

Research: scene-notes/research/s10-3b-curves.md.
Decision: scene-notes/decisions/s10-grade-component.md section 3b.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
