+### Curves
+
+*Scene-referred path only. Two 1D curves, applied in ACEScct **after** the ASC CDL and **before**
+the look LUT - which is the fixed order every on-set desk uses (Pomfort Livegrade: input transform
+-> CDL and saturation -> curves -> output transform). That position is also what keeps a capture's
+`.cdl` sidecar an exact description of the front of the chain. Every property here is animatable in
+TrackView.*
+
+**Neutral is free, not just cheap.** With every control at its default nothing is uploaded, nothing
+is bound and the shader issues no fetch - the picture is the same bits it would have been without
+this group.
+
+**Master (luma) curve.** One curve, applied identically to all three channels, so it shapes
+tonality and cannot introduce a colour cast - that is what the wheels are for. Five control points
+at fixed places in the grading space, each dialled as **an offset in stops**: 0 leaves that part of
+the picture exactly where it was.
+
+| Property | Range | Default | Where it sits | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Master Black (stops) | -4 to 4 (slider ±2) | 0 | ACEScct 0.0, ~7.2 stops under grey | The floor. Negative crushes the deepest blacks, positive lifts them. With Master Shadow this is where a film toe is made. |
+| Master Shadow (stops) | -4 to 4 | 0 | 4 stops under 18 % grey | The shadow half of an S-curve. |
+| Master Mid (stops) | -4 to 4 | 0 | **ACEScct(0.18) = 0.4136** | Moves the mid tone *without moving black or white* - something the aperture cannot do. |
+| Master Highlight (stops) | -4 to 4 | 0 | 4 stops over grey | The highlight half of an S-curve; pull it down instead for a film shoulder. |
+| Master White (stops) | -4 to 4 | 0 | ACEScct 1.0 = +10.3 stops | Above the output transform's own domain, so little of this is visible on screen - but it is in the EXR, because the export tap is taken after the curve. |
+
+Between the knots the curve is a **monotone cubic Hermite** (Fritsch-Carlson) - the interpolant
+that cannot overshoot, so a steep control point does not put ringing around a highlight. On
+collinear knots it collapses to the straight line exactly, which is why all-zero really is the
+identity and not merely close to it.
+
+Outside `[0, 1]` (i.e. above +10.3 stops, reachable with **Sensor Clip** off or high) the curve is
+extended with slope 1 rather than clamped, so a capture keeps the highlight separation the sensor
+recorded.
+
+**Sat vs Sat.** The output saturation **multiplier** as a function of how saturated the pixel
+already was - Resolve's control of the same name, with the same convention that 1 means unchanged.
+It is the tool for "everything gains life except what was already screaming": raise the middle and
+pull the top down.
+
+| Property | Range | Default | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Sat @ 0 (neutrals) | 0 to 4 (slider 0-2) | 1 | The near-neutral parts of the frame. Below 1 cleans a faint cast out of greys. |
+| Sat @ 25 % | 0 to 4 | 1 | Barely-tinted colours - skin, wood, weathered paint. |
+| Sat @ 50 % | 0 to 4 | 1 | Ordinary colours. |
+| Sat @ 75 % | 0 to 4 | 1 | Strong colours. |
+| Sat @ 100 % | 0 to 4 | 1 | The most saturated thing in the frame - a warning light, a red coat, a clipped neon. Pulling this down tames exactly those and leaves everything else alone, which no global saturation slider can do. |
+
+The x axis is measured in the working space as
+
+```
+luma = dot(c, (0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722))     <- the ASC CDL's own Rec.709 weights
+sat  = saturate(length(c - luma) * 2.0)
+out  = luma + (c - luma) * multiplier
+```
+
+so the axis is the **length of the very vector the multiplier scales**: push the curve to 0 at some
+saturation and you get exactly the grey the operator produces there. It is also
+**exposure-invariant** - the space is log and the weights sum to 1, so a stop shifts `c` and `luma`
+by the same amount and leaves `c - luma` untouched. `sat = 1` corresponds to 8.8 stops of spread
+between the extreme channels; for scale, an ordinary strong red reads about 0.54 and a near-primary
+about 0.96.
+
+**Why five numbers and not a curve you can drag.** CRYENGINE 5.7.1 does have a curve property row,
+and a reflected spline would draw here - but the only curve-editor panel in the whole Sandbox is
+built by the Particle Editor, so in the level editor the row has nothing to open. Five plain floats
+are typeable, readable, TrackView-animatable, and their neutral is a literal zero, which is what
+makes "untouched" an exact identity rather than a measurement.
+
+**They travel with a capture** as their own files - the master curve as a Resolve 1D `.cube` and
+both curves' control points as text, because the ASC CDL cannot carry either and a Sat vs Sat curve
+is not a LUT at all. See [docs/SceneReferredExport.md](docs/SceneReferredExport.md).
+
